<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The declaration of the "opencode serve" task.

  This file is the SOURCE OF TRUTH for the task: what runs it, when it runs,
  as whom, and how it has to survive. The scheduler module compares it with
  the live task and reports the difference; it never invents a task of its
  own.

  THE NAME. The file is OpenCodeServe.xml; the task on the hosts is called
  "OpenCode Serve" (lamet) and "opencode-serve" (vetymae). The file base name
  is the DECLARATION ID and the only source of truth for the name: the module
  matches a live task by the name's KEY (the name with case, spaces, dashes
  and underscores removed; Get-TaskNameKey in State.ps1) so all three
  spellings are one task. An alias table was the alternative and was
  rejected: it needs an entry per host and goes stale the day a third host
  spells it "opencode_serve". A live task that is NOT the same key but could
  still be this task (it runs the same launcher, or its name merely extends
  ours: OpenCodeServeOld) is reported as UNDECLARED by plan and status, never
  ignored. The URI below is the canonical spelling; Task Scheduler takes the
  registered name from the module, not from here.

  WHAT THE DIFF COMPARES: one dimension per field, each named in the
  report: UserId, LogonType, RunLevel, the trigger set, Command+Arguments,
  WorkingDirectory, MultipleInstances, DisallowStartIfOnBatteries,
  StopIfGoingOnBatteries, ExecutionTimeLimit, RestartOnFailure,
  StartWhenAvailable, Enabled. Everything else in a task document is noise;
  the list of ignored fields and the reasons are in
  src/modules/scheduler/State.ps1 and docs/scheduler.md.

  THE FOUR SETTINGS BELOW ARE THE ONES THAT BIT THE OWNER (2026-11-04). A
  laptop dropped off AC and its server died in the middle of the week: the
  live task said DisallowStartIfOnBatteries=True, StopIfGoingOnBatteries=True,
  ExecutionTimeLimit=PT72H, RestartCount=0, while this file said
  false/false/PT0S/5. A server meant to run for weeks must not be killed by
  the power state, must not be killed after three days, and must come back
  when it dies. StartWhenAvailable=true is the same story from the other
  side: a start missed while the machine was off is picked up when it is
  next available. If the machine disagrees, plan names the field.

  THE ACTION IS WHAT REALLY RUNS, NOT THE CANON. On the Windows hosts the
  server is started by Git Bash in LOGIN mode:

    bash -lc "bash <profile>/.local/share/metaagent/tools/host-scripts/opencode-serve.sh"

  `-lc` is load-bearing: a login shell sources ~/.bashrc, and that is where
  OPENCODE_SERVER_USERNAME / OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD are exported. The
  opencode process does not read service.json when its launcher was started
  from a profile-sourced shell, so without the password in the profile the
  server answers 401. A plain `-c` (the vetymae spelling) does not source the
  profile; that is DRIFT, and the diff reports it; the declaration does not
  imitate it.

  The launcher under src/host-scripts/ is the winos canon (self-sufficient,
  reads no profile) and is NOT what the task runs: on Windows the owner keeps
  the bashrc-based launcher, because that is where the password lives. See
  docs/opencode-registry.md, "the launcher decision".

  Placeholders (resolved per host by the scheduler module):

    __USER__        the account the task runs as, in Task Scheduler's
                    DOMAIN\user form
    __HOME_POSIX__  the user's profile in Git Bash form (/c/Users/oqyude),
                    which is how bash spells a Windows path in an argument
    __GIT_BASH__    bash.exe of Git for Windows; Program Files first, the
                    same order src/host-scripts/opencode-serve.cmd uses
    __DATA_ROOT__   the winos data root
    __TOOLS_DIR__   the host's tools directory
    __HOME__        the user's profile directory

  THE LOGON TYPE is the field that drifted between the two hosts:
  InteractiveToken (this file) against S4U on the other one. InteractiveToken
  is the safe choice for a server that has to reach the network. An S4U task
  runs without stored credentials and may be denied network access. Change
  one line to move to the other mode.
-->
<Task version="1.4" xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/windows/2004/02/mit/task">
  <RegistrationInfo>
    <Description>opencode serve on 0.0.0.0:4096, started from the winos task declaration.</Description>
    <URI>\OpenCode Serve</URI>
  </RegistrationInfo>
  <Triggers>
    <!--
      Both triggers on purpose. At logon is what actually starts the server
      under an InteractiveToken principal (there is no stored credential, so
      there is no session before somebody logs in); the boot trigger is what
      makes the intent explicit on a machine that has one, and it costs
      nothing: it simply cannot fire earlier than the logon.
    -->
    <BootTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    </BootTrigger>
    <LogonTrigger>
      <Enabled>true</Enabled>
      <UserId>__USER__</UserId>
    </LogonTrigger>
  </Triggers>
  <Principals>
    <Principal id="Author">
      <UserId>__USER__</UserId>
      <LogonType>InteractiveToken</LogonType>
      <!--
        RunLevel in the vocabulary of the XML, not of the PowerShell cmdlet:
        Task Scheduler writes "LeastPrivilege" for what
        Register-ScheduledTask -RunLevel Limited means. Declaring "Limited"
        here would make every live task differ from the declaration on a
        field that says nothing.
      -->
      <RunLevel>LeastPrivilege</RunLevel>
    </Principal>
  </Principals>
  <Settings>
    <MultipleInstancesPolicy>IgnoreNew</MultipleInstancesPolicy>
    <DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>false</DisallowStartIfOnBatteries>
    <StopIfGoingOnBatteries>false</StopIfGoingOnBatteries>
    <StartWhenAvailable>true</StartWhenAvailable>
    <Enabled>true</Enabled>
    <ExecutionTimeLimit>PT0S</ExecutionTimeLimit>
    <RestartOnFailure>
      <Interval>PT1M</Interval>
      <Count>5</Count>
    </RestartOnFailure>
  </Settings>
  <Actions Context="Author">
    <Exec>
      <Command>__GIT_BASH__</Command>
      <Arguments>-lc "bash __HOME_POSIX__/.local/share/metaagent/tools/host-scripts/opencode-serve.sh"</Arguments>
    </Exec>
  </Actions>
</Task>
